    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old female named Mildred with heart failure, diabetes, and moderate dementia is being discharged from the hospital to a skilled nursing facility for post-acute rehabilitation. The discharge team is implementing a structured care transitions program. Which of the following is the most important element of safe hospital-to-SNF care transition to prevent early readmission?', '["Providing the patient with a thick discharge folder of educational pamphlets and assuming the patient will manage their own care transition independently", "A structured medication reconciliation (accurate and complete transfer of the medication list), a detailed discharge summary transmitted to the SNF before or at the time of transfer, clear communication of pending tests and follow-up needs, and a follow-up phone call within 48\u201372 hours", "Scheduling the patient''s first post-discharge physician follow-up appointment in 3 months, as stable patients do not require early reassessment", "Instructing the SNF to restart all home medications from memory without providing a written reconciled medication list", "Using verbal-only handoff communication to the SNF nursing staff to save time, as written documentation is not required by The Joint Commission"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Hospital-to-SNF care transitions** are high-risk periods for medication errors, communication failures, and early readmission. Evidence-based transition elements include: (1) **Medication reconciliation** — the single most important element; errors are present in up to 60% of post-discharge medication lists; (2) **Complete discharge summary** — transferred to the SNF in advance of or at the time of admission; should include diagnosis, treatment, pending results, follow-up plans; (3) **Early follow-up** — phone call within 48–72 hours; physician visit within 7 days; (4) **Patient/family education** — teach-back method for warning signs; (5) **Care transition models** (Care Transitions Intervention by Coleman; BOOST; Project RED) have been shown to reduce 30-day readmissions by 20–30%. The CMS Transitional Care Management (TCM) billing codes incentivize structured post-discharge follow-up. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The medical director of a skilled nursing facility in Illinois, Dr. Harry, is restructuring the facility''s care model to use a comprehensive interdisciplinary team approach. Which of the following best describes the appropriate roles of each interdisciplinary team member in the long-term care setting?', '["Physical therapists in nursing homes provide exclusively exercise instruction classes for groups of residents and do not conduct individual patient assessments", "Social workers in nursing homes are limited to billing and insurance verification tasks and have no clinical role in patient assessment or care planning", "The physician alone should make all care decisions without input from nursing, social work, or therapy disciplines, as other team members have no clinical decision-making authority in nursing home settings", "The interdisciplinary team includes the physician/NP/PA (medical management), registered nurse (skilled care, care plan coordination), social worker (psychosocial assessment, discharge planning, family communication), physical therapist (mobility and strength), occupational therapist (ADL function and home safety), speech-language pathologist (swallowing and communication), dietitian (nutritional assessment), and activities coordinator (recreational and cognitive engagement)", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman is a full member of the clinical care team and attends all care plan meetings as a co-treating provider"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **interdisciplinary team (IDT)** model is mandated in Medicare/Medicaid-certified nursing homes by **OBRA 1987** and is the standard of care in geriatric settings. Federal regulations require a formal care plan meeting with the IDT within 14 days of admission and at least quarterly thereafter. Team composition and roles: | Member | Primary Role | | --- | --- | | **Physician/NP/PA** | Medical diagnosis and management | | **Registered Nurse** | Skilled care delivery, care plan coordination | | **Social Worker** | Psychosocial assessment, family communication, discharge planning, resident rights advocacy | | **Physical Therapist** | Mobility, gait, strength, fall prevention | | **Occupational Therapist** | ADL function, home/facility safety, adaptive equipment | | **Speech-Language Pathologist** | Swallowing, communication | | **Registered Dietitian** | Nutritional assessment and interventions | | **Activities/Recreation Coordinator** | Cognitive and recreational engagement | (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The medical director of a skilled nursing facility in Illinois, Dr. Gertrude, is restructuring the facility''s care model to use a comprehensive interdisciplinary team approach. Which of the following best describes the appropriate roles of each interdisciplinary team member in the long-term care setting?', '["Social workers in nursing homes are limited to billing and insurance verification tasks and have no clinical role in patient assessment or care planning", "The interdisciplinary team includes the physician/NP/PA (medical management), registered nurse (skilled care, care plan coordination), social worker (psychosocial assessment, discharge planning, family communication), physical therapist (mobility and strength), occupational therapist (ADL function and home safety), speech-language pathologist (swallowing and communication), dietitian (nutritional assessment), and activities coordinator (recreational and cognitive engagement)", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman is a full member of the clinical care team and attends all care plan meetings as a co-treating provider", "Physical therapists in nursing homes provide exclusively exercise instruction classes for groups of residents and do not conduct individual patient assessments", "The physician alone should make all care decisions without input from nursing, social work, or therapy disciplines, as other team members have no clinical decision-making authority in nursing home settings"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **interdisciplinary team (IDT)** model is mandated in Medicare/Medicaid-certified nursing homes by **OBRA 1987** and is the standard of care in geriatric settings. Federal regulations require a formal care plan meeting with the IDT within 14 days of admission and at least quarterly thereafter. Team composition and roles: | Member | Primary Role | | --- | --- | | **Physician/NP/PA** | Medical diagnosis and management | | **Registered Nurse** | Skilled care delivery, care plan coordination | | **Social Worker** | Psychosocial assessment, family communication, discharge planning, resident rights advocacy | | **Physical Therapist** | Mobility, gait, strength, fall prevention | | **Occupational Therapist** | ADL function, home/facility safety, adaptive equipment | | **Speech-Language Pathologist** | Swallowing, communication | | **Registered Dietitian** | Nutritional assessment and interventions | | **Activities/Recreation Coordinator** | Cognitive and recreational engagement | (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The medical director of a skilled nursing facility in California, Dr. James, is restructuring the facility''s care model to use a comprehensive interdisciplinary team approach. Which of the following best describes the appropriate roles of each interdisciplinary team member in the long-term care setting?', '["The Long-Term Care Ombudsman is a full member of the clinical care team and attends all care plan meetings as a co-treating provider", "The physician alone should make all care decisions without input from nursing, social work, or therapy disciplines, as other team members have no clinical decision-making authority in nursing home settings", "The interdisciplinary team includes the physician/NP/PA (medical management), registered nurse (skilled care, care plan coordination), social worker (psychosocial assessment, discharge planning, family communication), physical therapist (mobility and strength), occupational therapist (ADL function and home safety), speech-language pathologist (swallowing and communication), dietitian (nutritional assessment), and activities coordinator (recreational and cognitive engagement)", "Physical therapists in nursing homes provide exclusively exercise instruction classes for groups of residents and do not conduct individual patient assessments", "Social workers in nursing homes are limited to billing and insurance verification tasks and have no clinical role in patient assessment or care planning"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **interdisciplinary team (IDT)** model is mandated in Medicare/Medicaid-certified nursing homes by **OBRA 1987** and is the standard of care in geriatric settings. Federal regulations require a formal care plan meeting with the IDT within 14 days of admission and at least quarterly thereafter. Team composition and roles: | Member | Primary Role | | --- | --- | | **Physician/NP/PA** | Medical diagnosis and management | | **Registered Nurse** | Skilled care delivery, care plan coordination | | **Social Worker** | Psychosocial assessment, family communication, discharge planning, resident rights advocacy | | **Physical Therapist** | Mobility, gait, strength, fall prevention | | **Occupational Therapist** | ADL function, home/facility safety, adaptive equipment | | **Speech-Language Pathologist** | Swallowing, communication | | **Registered Dietitian** | Nutritional assessment and interventions | | **Activities/Recreation Coordinator** | Cognitive and recreational engagement | (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The medical director of a skilled nursing facility in Pennsylvania, Dr. Martha, is restructuring the facility''s care model to use a comprehensive interdisciplinary team approach. Which of the following best describes the appropriate roles of each interdisciplinary team member in the long-term care setting?', '["The interdisciplinary team includes the physician/NP/PA (medical management), registered nurse (skilled care, care plan coordination), social worker (psychosocial assessment, discharge planning, family communication), physical therapist (mobility and strength), occupational therapist (ADL function and home safety), speech-language pathologist (swallowing and communication), dietitian (nutritional assessment), and activities coordinator (recreational and cognitive engagement)", "The physician alone should make all care decisions without input from nursing, social work, or therapy disciplines, as other team members have no clinical decision-making authority in nursing home settings", "Physical therapists in nursing homes provide exclusively exercise instruction classes for groups of residents and do not conduct individual patient assessments", "Social workers in nursing homes are limited to billing and insurance verification tasks and have no clinical role in patient assessment or care planning", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman is a full member of the clinical care team and attends all care plan meetings as a co-treating provider"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **interdisciplinary team (IDT)** model is mandated in Medicare/Medicaid-certified nursing homes by **OBRA 1987** and is the standard of care in geriatric settings. Federal regulations require a formal care plan meeting with the IDT within 14 days of admission and at least quarterly thereafter. Team composition and roles: | Member | Primary Role | | --- | --- | | **Physician/NP/PA** | Medical diagnosis and management | | **Registered Nurse** | Skilled care delivery, care plan coordination | | **Social Worker** | Psychosocial assessment, family communication, discharge planning, resident rights advocacy | | **Physical Therapist** | Mobility, gait, strength, fall prevention | | **Occupational Therapist** | ADL function, home/facility safety, adaptive equipment | | **Speech-Language Pathologist** | Swallowing, communication | | **Registered Dietitian** | Nutritional assessment and interventions | | **Activities/Recreation Coordinator** | Cognitive and recreational engagement | (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The medical director of a skilled nursing facility in Ohio, Dr. Stanley, is restructuring the facility''s care model to use a comprehensive interdisciplinary team approach. Which of the following best describes the appropriate roles of each interdisciplinary team member in the long-term care setting?', '["Physical therapists in nursing homes provide exclusively exercise instruction classes for groups of residents and do not conduct individual patient assessments", "The physician alone should make all care decisions without input from nursing, social work, or therapy disciplines, as other team members have no clinical decision-making authority in nursing home settings", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman is a full member of the clinical care team and attends all care plan meetings as a co-treating provider", "Social workers in nursing homes are limited to billing and insurance verification tasks and have no clinical role in patient assessment or care planning", "The interdisciplinary team includes the physician/NP/PA (medical management), registered nurse (skilled care, care plan coordination), social worker (psychosocial assessment, discharge planning, family communication), physical therapist (mobility and strength), occupational therapist (ADL function and home safety), speech-language pathologist (swallowing and communication), dietitian (nutritional assessment), and activities coordinator (recreational and cognitive engagement)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **interdisciplinary team (IDT)** model is mandated in Medicare/Medicaid-certified nursing homes by **OBRA 1987** and is the standard of care in geriatric settings. Federal regulations require a formal care plan meeting with the IDT within 14 days of admission and at least quarterly thereafter. Team composition and roles: | Member | Primary Role | | --- | --- | | **Physician/NP/PA** | Medical diagnosis and management | | **Registered Nurse** | Skilled care delivery, care plan coordination | | **Social Worker** | Psychosocial assessment, family communication, discharge planning, resident rights advocacy | | **Physical Therapist** | Mobility, gait, strength, fall prevention | | **Occupational Therapist** | ADL function, home/facility safety, adaptive equipment | | **Speech-Language Pathologist** | Swallowing, communication | | **Registered Dietitian** | Nutritional assessment and interventions | | **Activities/Recreation Coordinator** | Cognitive and recreational engagement | (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
