orker** | Psychosocial assessment, family communication, discharge planning, resident rights advocacy | | **Physical Therapist** | Mobility, gait, strength, fall prevention | | **Occupational Therapist** | ADL function, home/facility safety, adaptive equipment | | **Speech-Language Pathologist** | Swallowing, communication | | **Registered Dietitian** | Nutritional assessment and interventions | | **Activities/Recreation Coordinator** | Cognitive and recreational engagement | (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The medical director of a skilled nursing facility in New York, Dr. Mildred, is restructuring the facility''s care model to use a comprehensive interdisciplinary team approach. Which of the following best describes the appropriate roles of each interdisciplinary team member in the long-term care setting?', '["Physical therapists in nursing homes provide exclusively exercise instruction classes for groups of residents and do not conduct individual patient assessments", "The interdisciplinary team includes the physician/NP/PA (medical management), registered nurse (skilled care, care plan coordination), social worker (psychosocial assessment, discharge planning, family communication), physical therapist (mobility and strength), occupational therapist (ADL function and home safety), speech-language pathologist (swallowing and communication), dietitian (nutritional assessment), and activities coordinator (recreational and cognitive engagement)", "The physician alone should make all care decisions without input from nursing, social work, or therapy disciplines, as other team members have no clinical decision-making authority in nursing home settings", "Social workers in nursing homes are limited to billing and insurance verification tasks and have no clinical role in patient assessment or care planning", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman is a full member of the clinical care team and attends all care plan meetings as a co-treating provider"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **interdisciplinary team (IDT)** model is mandated in Medicare/Medicaid-certified nursing homes by **OBRA 1987** and is the standard of care in geriatric settings. Federal regulations require a formal care plan meeting with the IDT within 14 days of admission and at least quarterly thereafter. Team composition and roles: | Member | Primary Role | | --- | --- | | **Physician/NP/PA** | Medical diagnosis and management | | **Registered Nurse** | Skilled care delivery, care plan coordination | | **Social Worker** | Psychosocial assessment, family communication, discharge planning, resident rights advocacy | | **Physical Therapist** | Mobility, gait, strength, fall prevention | | **Occupational Therapist** | ADL function, home/facility safety, adaptive equipment | | **Speech-Language Pathologist** | Swallowing, communication | | **Registered Dietitian** | Nutritional assessment and interventions | | **Activities/Recreation Coordinator** | Cognitive and recreational engagement | (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A family member contacts the clinic of a 71-year-old male named Bernard residing in a nursing home. They report that Bernard has been complaining of not receiving ordered meals, being left in soiled clothes for hours, and being told by staff that reporting complaints ''will make things worse.'' The family is unsure how to address this with the facility. Which of the following correctly describes the role and authority of the Long-Term Care Ombudsman in this situation?', '["The Long-Term Care Ombudsman has authority to immediately arrest nursing home staff found to be in violation of resident care standards", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman program is a hospital-based service that exclusively resolves complaints about acute care settings and has no jurisdiction over nursing homes", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman program only accepts complaints about financial billing disputes; it has no authority to investigate issues related to care quality, dignity, or neglect", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman is a private attorney who charges hourly legal fees to represent nursing home residents in civil litigation against facilities", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman (LTCO), established by the Older Americans Act, is a state and locally-funded advocate who investigates and resolves complaints made by or on behalf of nursing home residents, with authority to access the facility, interview staff and residents, review records, and refer for regulatory action \u2014 all at no cost to the resident or family"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **Long-Term Care Ombudsman (LTCO) Program** is established under **Title VII of the Older Americans Act** and is administered at the state and local level. Every state is required to have an LTCO program. Key functions: (1) **Advocate** for residents of nursing homes and assisted living facilities; (2) **Investigate complaints** about care quality, dignity violations, abuse, neglect, and rights infringements; (3) **Access authority**: Ombudsmen have the legal right to enter facilities and review records (with resident consent); (4) **Free service** to residents and families; (5) **Referral authority**: can refer cases to the State Survey Agency for regulatory action or to Adult Protective Services; (6) **Regular facility visits**: provide monitoring and early problem detection. The LTCO is distinct from APS (which handles community-based abuse) and the State Survey Agency (which conducts formal inspections). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
