    (v_topic_id, 'A family member contacts the clinic of a 71-year-old male named David residing in a nursing home. They report that David has been complaining of not receiving ordered meals, being left in soiled clothes for hours, and being told by staff that reporting complaints ''will make things worse.'' The family is unsure how to address this with the facility. Which of the following correctly describes the role and authority of the Long-Term Care Ombudsman in this situation?', '["The Long-Term Care Ombudsman program is a hospital-based service that exclusively resolves complaints about acute care settings and has no jurisdiction over nursing homes", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman program only accepts complaints about financial billing disputes; it has no authority to investigate issues related to care quality, dignity, or neglect", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman has authority to immediately arrest nursing home staff found to be in violation of resident care standards", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman (LTCO), established by the Older Americans Act, is a state and locally-funded advocate who investigates and resolves complaints made by or on behalf of nursing home residents, with authority to access the facility, interview staff and residents, review records, and refer for regulatory action \u2014 all at no cost to the resident or family", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman is a private attorney who charges hourly legal fees to represent nursing home residents in civil litigation against facilities"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **Long-Term Care Ombudsman (LTCO) Program** is established under **Title VII of the Older Americans Act** and is administered at the state and local level. Every state is required to have an LTCO program. Key functions: (1) **Advocate** for residents of nursing homes and assisted living facilities; (2) **Investigate complaints** about care quality, dignity violations, abuse, neglect, and rights infringements; (3) **Access authority**: Ombudsmen have the legal right to enter facilities and review records (with resident consent); (4) **Free service** to residents and families; (5) **Referral authority**: can refer cases to the State Survey Agency for regulatory action or to Adult Protective Services; (6) **Regular facility visits**: provide monitoring and early problem detection. The LTCO is distinct from APS (which handles community-based abuse) and the State Survey Agency (which conducts formal inspections). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A family member contacts the clinic of a 83-year-old male named Robert residing in a nursing home. They report that Robert has been complaining of not receiving ordered meals, being left in soiled clothes for hours, and being told by staff that reporting complaints ''will make things worse.'' The family is unsure how to address this with the facility. Which of the following correctly describes the role and authority of the Long-Term Care Ombudsman in this situation?', '["The Long-Term Care Ombudsman (LTCO), established by the Older Americans Act, is a state and locally-funded advocate who investigates and resolves complaints made by or on behalf of nursing home residents, with authority to access the facility, interview staff and residents, review records, and refer for regulatory action \u2014 all at no cost to the resident or family", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman has authority to immediately arrest nursing home staff found to be in violation of resident care standards", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman program only accepts complaints about financial billing disputes; it has no authority to investigate issues related to care quality, dignity, or neglect", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman program is a hospital-based service that exclusively resolves complaints about acute care settings and has no jurisdiction over nursing homes", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman is a private attorney who charges hourly legal fees to represent nursing home residents in civil litigation against facilities"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **Long-Term Care Ombudsman (LTCO) Program** is established under **Title VII of the Older Americans Act** and is administered at the state and local level. Every state is required to have an LTCO program. Key functions: (1) **Advocate** for residents of nursing homes and assisted living facilities; (2) **Investigate complaints** about care quality, dignity violations, abuse, neglect, and rights infringements; (3) **Access authority**: Ombudsmen have the legal right to enter facilities and review records (with resident consent); (4) **Free service** to residents and families; (5) **Referral authority**: can refer cases to the State Survey Agency for regulatory action or to Adult Protective Services; (6) **Regular facility visits**: provide monitoring and early problem detection. The LTCO is distinct from APS (which handles community-based abuse) and the State Survey Agency (which conducts formal inspections). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A primary care geriatrician is implementing a standardized social determinants of health (SDOH) screening program for all older adult patients. A 87-year-old male named Kenneth screens positive for food insecurity, housing instability, lack of transportation to medical appointments, social isolation, and inability to pay utility bills. Which of the following represents the most evidence-based and comprehensive clinical response to a positive SDOH screening?', '["Immediately hospitalize the patient for all identified social needs, as the inpatient setting is the most efficient place to resolve food insecurity and housing instability", "Prescribe a multi-vitamin and high-calorie supplement as the sole intervention for food insecurity and dismiss the patient with no further SDOH follow-up", "Tell the patient that social issues are outside the scope of clinical medicine and that they should contact a charity organization independently", "Refer the patient to the emergency department for all social needs, as only emergency medicine physicians are qualified to address social determinants of health", "Document identified SDOH needs in the problem list; refer to a licensed clinical social worker and community health worker for individualized resource navigation; connect with the Area Agency on Aging for benefit enrollment assistance; and follow up within 30 days to assess whether connections to resources were successfully made"]'::jsonb, 4, '**SDOH screening tools** such as **PRAPARE** (Protocol for Responding to and Assessing Patients'' Assets, Risks, and Experiences), **AHC HRSN Screening Tool** (developed by CMS), and the **WellRx** address domains including: food insecurity, housing instability, transportation, social isolation, financial strain, and utility assistance. Positive screens require action beyond documentation: (1) **Social work/community health worker (CHW) referral** — CHWs are trusted, culturally competent community liaisons trained in resource navigation; (2) **Area Agency on Aging** — comprehensive benefit enrollment, Meals on Wheels, transportation, case management; (3) **Electronic resource platforms** (findhelp.org, 211) — can connect patients to local food pantries, LIHEAP, SNAP; (4) **Follow-up to close the loop** — screening without follow-up is insufficient; systems-level referral tracking is needed. This is a growing priority in Medicare quality programs (CMMI health equity initiatives). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
