source navigation; (2) **Area Agency on Aging** — comprehensive benefit enrollment, Meals on Wheels, transportation, case management; (3) **Electronic resource platforms** (findhelp.org, 211) — can connect patients to local food pantries, LIHEAP, SNAP; (4) **Follow-up to close the loop** — screening without follow-up is insufficient; systems-level referral tracking is needed. This is a growing priority in Medicare quality programs (CMMI health equity initiatives). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A primary care geriatrician is implementing a standardized social determinants of health (SDOH) screening program for all older adult patients. A 95-year-old female named Jean screens positive for food insecurity, housing instability, lack of transportation to medical appointments, social isolation, and inability to pay utility bills. Which of the following represents the most evidence-based and comprehensive clinical response to a positive SDOH screening?', '["Document identified SDOH needs in the problem list; refer to a licensed clinical social worker and community health worker for individualized resource navigation; connect with the Area Agency on Aging for benefit enrollment assistance; and follow up within 30 days to assess whether connections to resources were successfully made", "Refer the patient to the emergency department for all social needs, as only emergency medicine physicians are qualified to address social determinants of health", "Prescribe a multi-vitamin and high-calorie supplement as the sole intervention for food insecurity and dismiss the patient with no further SDOH follow-up", "Immediately hospitalize the patient for all identified social needs, as the inpatient setting is the most efficient place to resolve food insecurity and housing instability", "Tell the patient that social issues are outside the scope of clinical medicine and that they should contact a charity organization independently"]'::jsonb, 0, '**SDOH screening tools** such as **PRAPARE** (Protocol for Responding to and Assessing Patients'' Assets, Risks, and Experiences), **AHC HRSN Screening Tool** (developed by CMS), and the **WellRx** address domains including: food insecurity, housing instability, transportation, social isolation, financial strain, and utility assistance. Positive screens require action beyond documentation: (1) **Social work/community health worker (CHW) referral** — CHWs are trusted, culturally competent community liaisons trained in resource navigation; (2) **Area Agency on Aging** — comprehensive benefit enrollment, Meals on Wheels, transportation, case management; (3) **Electronic resource platforms** (findhelp.org, 211) — can connect patients to local food pantries, LIHEAP, SNAP; (4) **Follow-up to close the loop** — screening without follow-up is insufficient; systems-level referral tracking is needed. This is a growing priority in Medicare quality programs (CMMI health equity initiatives). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 17; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old male named Earl attends his Medicare Annual Wellness Visit (AWV). During the visit, the clinician is expected to assess the patient''s health status and social needs. Which of the following accurately describes a required or recommended component of the Medicare Annual Wellness Visit that directly addresses social determinants of health and aging-related risks?', '["The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit may only be performed in hospital outpatient settings by board-certified geriatricians and cannot be delivered by primary care physicians or nurse practitioners", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit requires a review of the patient''s functional status, cognition (using a structured screen), fall risk, depression screening (PHQ-9 or equivalent), advance care planning discussion, and social history including caregiver support, living situation, and community resource needs", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit requires a full physical examination (auscultation, percussion, and palpation of all organ systems) and a complete blood count, metabolic panel, and urinalysis for all patients", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit is a one-time benefit available only to Medicare beneficiaries in the first year of Medicare enrollment and cannot be repeated annually", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit is identical to a standard sick visit and consists solely of evaluating the chief complaint with no preventive or social assessment components"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **Medicare Annual Wellness Visit (AWV)** (established under the ACA in 2011) is an annual preventive visit covered at **100% under Medicare Part B** (no patient copay). Required components include: (1) **Health Risk Assessment (HRA)** — self-reported health status, psychosocial risks, behavioral risks; (2) **Cognitive assessment** — structured screen (MMSE, MoCA, or validated alternative); (3) **Depression screening** (PHQ-9 or equivalent); (4) **Fall risk assessment**; (5) **Functional status review** (ADLs); (6) **Advance care planning** discussion (documented); (7) **Social history** — living situation, caregiver support, community resources; (8) **Personalized prevention plan** — tailored screening, referrals, and health goals; (9) **Review of medications** and health providers. Key distinction: AWV does NOT require a full physical exam (that is a separate ''Welcome to Medicare'' preventive visit in year 1). It CAN be performed by any qualified clinician. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; CMS AWV Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 17)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old female named Grace attends her Medicare Annual Wellness Visit (AWV). During the visit, the clinician is expected to assess the patient''s health status and social needs. Which of the following accurately describes a required or recommended component of the Medicare Annual Wellness Visit that directly addresses social determinants of health and aging-related risks?', '["The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit is a one-time benefit available only to Medicare beneficiaries in the first year of Medicare enrollment and cannot be repeated annually", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit may only be performed in hospital outpatient settings by board-certified geriatricians and cannot be delivered by primary care physicians or nurse practitioners", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit is identical to a standard sick visit and consists solely of evaluating the chief complaint with no preventive or social assessment components", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit requires a full physical examination (auscultation, percussion, and palpation of all organ systems) and a complete blood count, metabolic panel, and urinalysis for all patients", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit requires a review of the patient''s functional status, cognition (using a structured screen), fall risk, depression screening (PHQ-9 or equivalent), advance care planning discussion, and social history including caregiver support, living situation, and community resource needs"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **Medicare Annual Wellness Visit (AWV)** (established under the ACA in 2011) is an annual preventive visit covered at **100% under Medicare Part B** (no patient copay). Required components include: (1) **Health Risk Assessment (HRA)** — self-reported health status, psychosocial risks, behavioral risks; (2) **Cognitive assessment** — structured screen (MMSE, MoCA, or validated alternative); (3) **Depression screening** (PHQ-9 or equivalent); (4) **Fall risk assessment**; (5) **Functional status review** (ADLs); (6) **Advance care planning** discussion (documented); (7) **Social history** — living situation, caregiver support, community resources; (8) **Personalized prevention plan** — tailored screening, referrals, and health goals; (9) **Review of medications** and health providers. Key distinction: AWV does NOT require a full physical exam (that is a separate ''Welcome to Medicare'' preventive visit in year 1). It CAN be performed by any qualified clinician. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; CMS AWV Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 17)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old female named Florence attends her Medicare Annual Wellness Visit (AWV). During the visit, the clinician is expected to assess the patient''s health status and social needs. Which of the following accurately describes a required or recommended component of the Medicare Annual Wellness Visit that directly addresses social determinants of health and aging-related risks?', '["The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit is a one-time benefit available only to Medicare beneficiaries in the first year of Medicare enrollment and cannot be repeated annually", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit requires a review of the patient''s functional status, cognition (using a structured screen), fall risk, depression screening (PHQ-9 or equivalent), advance care planning discussion, and social history including caregiver support, living situation, and community resource needs", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit may only be performed in hospital outpatient settings by board-certified geriatricians and cannot be delivered by primary care physicians or nurse practitioners", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit requires a full physical examination (auscultation, percussion, and palpation of all organ systems) and a complete blood count, metabolic panel, and urinalysis for all patients", "The Medicare Annual Wellness Visit is identical to a standard sick visit and consists solely of evaluating the chief complaint with no preventive or social assessment components"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **Medicare Annual Wellness Visit (AWV)** (established under the ACA in 2011) is an annual preventive visit covered at **100% under Medicare Part B** (no patient copay). Required components include: (1) **Health Risk Assessment (HRA)** — self-reported health status, psychosocial risks, behavioral risks; (2) **Cognitive assessment** — structured screen (MMSE, MoCA, or validated alternative); (3) **Depression screening** (PHQ-9 or equivalent); (4) **Fall risk assessment**; (5) **Functional status review** (ADLs); (6) **Advance care planning** discussion (documented); (7) **Social history** — living situation, caregiver support, community resources; (8) **Personalized prevention plan** — tailored screening, referrals, and health goals; (9) **Review of medications** and health providers. Key distinction: AWV does NOT require a full physical exam (that is a separate ''Welcome to Medicare'' preventive visit in year 1). It CAN be performed by any qualified clinician. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; CMS AWV Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 17)'),
